prompt:
Implement a 3D Snake game using Three.js (20×20×20 grid, six-directional movement, wrap-around boundaries).

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Grid and Boundaries]
(1) Use a 20×20×20 integer grid (the coordinate range must be fixed in the implementation, such as 0..19 or -10..9, must be consistent with __3D_STATE__, and must be explained in a comment);
(2) Indicate the boundaries using a wireframe or translucent planes; align the snake body and food with grid-cell centers;
(3) Walls: use wrap-around behavior. When the snake's head exits through one side, it enters from the opposite side; crossing a boundary must not cause death;

[Movement and Controls]
(4) Use a fixed movement interval (such as one step every 0.25s). The direction must be an integer vector {x,y,z}, and the snake must move one grid cell per step;
(5) WASD controls movement in the XZ plane: W/S/A/D correspond respectively to the documented Z± and X± directions (any mapping consistent with the scene orientation is acceptable, but it must be consistent with __3D_STATE__.direction);
(6) Space: Y+1; Shift: Y-1;
(7) Prevent immediate reversal that would cause self-collision, as in classic Snake;

[Food and Growth]
(8) Food: use a randomly colored SphereGeometry, placed randomly in an unoccupied grid cell;
(9) When the snake's head occupies the same grid cell as the food: increase the score by 10, increase the snake's length by 1 (add one segment), and spawn food in a new unoccupied cell;

[Failure]
(10) If the snake's head occupies the same grid cell as any of its own segments: set gameOver=true and stop movement;
(11) Only self-collision causes death; wrap-around boundary crossing must not cause death;

[UI]
(12) Use id="score" for the score; game status may be displayed using text with id="status" or similar;

[State Exposure (Required)]
(13) Update window.__3D_STATE__ on every step or every frame:
  - snakeLength: number
  - score: number
  - direction: { x, y, z } (integer unit vector)
  - headPos: { x, y, z } (integer grid coordinates)
  - foodPos: { x, y, z }
  - gameOver: boolean

[Evaluation Helpers (Recommended)]
(14) window.__STATEPROBE_simulateEat__(): equivalent to actually eating food; window.__STATEPROBE_triggerSelfCollision__(): make the next state transition enter self-collision gameOver.

Output a single HTML file that runs when opened directly in a browser.
